    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Recent geological and geophysical studies in regions of crustal extension have documented domains of tilted crustal blocks in the hanging wall above detachment surfaces (Proffett, 1977; Gans and Miller, 1983; Wernicke and Burchfiel, 1982; Okaya and Thompson, 1986; Allmendinger et. al., 1983). These observations have led to a plethora of physical model studies to try to understand the progressive development, kinematics and mechanics of this deformation style (McClay, 1990; McClayand Ellis, 1987; Vendeville, 1987; Vendeville et. al., 1987). These physical models have proven useful in helping to understand the necessary boundary conditions and rheologies needed to develop some structures. However,although experimentalists can specify boundary conditions and may understand the material behaviors of their modeling materials, an understanding of the physics leading to the formation of the evolving structures in the model apparatus can be quite complicated. For this reason, computer models may prove to be more instructive for understanding how and why structures form in extensional terranes.\r\n\r\nThe computer models presented here and the code that we have developed are based on the distinct element method (DEM). DEMis a discontinuum modeling approach for simulating systems of blocks undergoing deformation. The basic algorithm involves linear force-displacement relationships which are prescribed at block boundaries and block motion which is governed by Newton’s second law. As with the sandboxstudies, the brittle crust is represented by a granular material. Each particle of the granular assemblage has a radius between 15 and 20 m. These large \"particles\" might represent blocks of rock bounded by weak bedding planes, joints, and/or small faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
